**Key idea:** An inline function is expanded at the call site by the compiler (the body replaces the call), removing call/return overhead. Good for small, frequently called functions; the `inline` keyword is only a hint.

### Code (C)
```c
#include <stdio.h>
static inline int max(int a, int b) { return (a > b) ? a : b; }

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 7;
    printf("%d\n", max(x, y));   /* compiler may emit: (x > y) ? x : y */
    return 0;
}
```

| Aspect | Inline function | Normal call | Macro (`#define`) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Call overhead | none | push/jump/return | none |
| Type checking | yes | yes | no |
| Args evaluated once | yes | yes | no (`MAX(i++, j)` bug) |
| Code size | grows if large/used often | small | grows |
| Debuggable | yes | yes | hard |

### Follow-ups
- *When won't the compiler inline?* Recursive or large functions, or when the address is taken.

**Key idea:** `volatile` tells the compiler the variable can change outside the program's visible control flow (hardware registers, ISRs, other threads), so **every access must really read/write memory** – no caching in registers, no removal of "redundant" accesses, no reordering among volatile accesses.

### Code (polling an MMIO status register)
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#define UART_STATUS (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40001000u)
#define TX_READY    (1u << 0)

void wait_tx_ready(void) {
    while ((UART_STATUS & TX_READY) == 0) {
        /* without volatile the compiler may read once and loop forever */
    }
}
```

| Without volatile | With volatile |
| --- | --- |
| load once, spin on register copy | reload the address every iteration |
| back-to-back writes to a FIFO register merged | every write performed |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `volatile` is **not** a synchronization primitive: it does not make operations atomic or add memory barriers for multi-core.

### Follow-ups
- *Can a variable be const volatile?* Yes – e.g., a read-only status register: the program can't write it, but hardware changes it.

**Key idea:** A NULL pointer has value **0** (points to nothing). Its **size is the size of any pointer on that machine**: 4 bytes on a 32-bit system, 8 bytes on a 64-bit system.

### Code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    int *a = NULL;
    printf("value=%p size=%zu\n", (void *)a, sizeof(a));  /* (nil)/0x0, 8 on LP64 */
    if (a == NULL) printf("don't dereference me\n");
    return 0;
}
```

| Platform | sizeof(int *) |
| --- | --- |
| 32-bit (ILP32) | 4 |
| 64-bit (LP64) | 8 |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Dereferencing NULL → segmentation fault. Always check `malloc` results.
- SV analogue: `null` handle; calling a method on a null handle is a fatal runtime error – common in UVM when `create()` was forgotten.

**Key idea:** A linked list is a sequence of nodes where each node holds data and a link (pointer) to the next node. Types: **singly linked** (next only), **doubly linked** (next and prev), and **circular** variants of either.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  H["head"] --> N1["10 | next"] --> N2["20 | next"] --> N3["30 | NULL"]
```

### Code (C – singly linked list)
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct node { int data; struct node *next; } node_t;

node_t *push_front(node_t *head, int v) {
    node_t *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
    n->data = v; n->next = head;
    return n;                      /* new head */
}
node_t *find(node_t *h, int v) {
    while (h && h->data != v) h = h->next;
    return h;
}
```

| Type | Links | Traversal | Delete given node pointer |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Singly | next | forward | O(n) (need prev) |
| Doubly | next, prev | both ways | O(1) |
| Circular | last → first | loop | depends |

### Follow-ups
- *Array vs list?* List: O(1) insert/delete at a known node, O(n) indexed access, poor cache locality.

**Key idea:** Space complexity counts memory needed as a function of input size. Distinguish **total** (including the input, as the book counts) from **auxiliary** (extra) space.

| Algorithm | Book (total) | Auxiliary space |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Linear search | O(1) | O(1) |
| Binary search | O(1) | O(1) iterative (O(log N) recursive) |
| Insertion sort | O(N) | O(1) – in place |
| Merge sort | O(N) | O(N) – merge buffer |
| Bucket sort | O(N) | O(N + k) – k buckets |

### Code (in-place insertion sort – O(1) extra)
```c
void insertion_sort(int *a, int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        int key = a[i], j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }
        a[j + 1] = key;
    }
}
```

### Follow-ups
- *In-place O(N log N) sort?* Heap sort (O(1) aux); quicksort uses O(log N) stack on average.

**Key idea:** A union is as large as its **largest** member (rounded up to the strictest alignment): max(4, 8) = **8 bytes**.

| Bytes 0–3 | Bytes 4–7 |
| --- | --- |
| IntID **or** CharID[0..3] | CharID[4..7] |

```c
#include <stdio.h>
union ID { int IntID; char CharID[8]; };
int main(void) {
    union ID u;
    u.IntID = 0x41424344;
    printf("%zu %c\n", sizeof u, u.CharID[0]);   /* 8, 'D' on little-endian */
    return 0;
}
```

### Follow-ups
- *Why 'D'?* Writing IntID overwrote CharID[0..3]; on a little-endian machine byte 0 is 0x44.

**Key idea:** **Practical Extraction and Report(ing) Language** – created by Larry Wall (1987) for text processing and report generation.

| Strength | Typical DV use |
| --- | --- |
| Regular expressions built in | parse simulation logs, extract errors |
| Hashes/arrays | regression bookkeeping |
| Text generation | generate register/RTL/testbench code from specs |
| CPAN modules | everything else |

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict; use warnings;
my %count;
while (<>) { $count{$1}++ if /^(UVM_ERROR|UVM_FATAL)/; }
printf "%-10s %d\n", $_, $count{$_} for sort keys %count;
```

**Key idea:** A binary tree is a linked structure where each node has data and up to **two children** (left and right pointers). In a **binary search tree** (BST), left subtree < node < right subtree, giving O(log n) search when balanced.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
  N8["8"] --> N3["3"]
  N8 --> N10["10"]
  N3 --> N1["1"]
  N3 --> N6["6"]
  N10 --> N14["14"]
```

### Code (C – BST insert/search)
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct node { int key; struct node *left, *right; } node_t;

node_t *insert(node_t *r, int k) {
    if (!r) { r = calloc(1, sizeof *r); r->key = k; return r; }
    if (k < r->key) r->left = insert(r->left, k);
    else            r->right = insert(r->right, k);
    return r;
}
node_t *search(node_t *r, int k) {
    while (r && r->key != k) r = (k < r->key) ? r->left : r->right;
    return r;
}
```

| Traversal | Order for tree above |
| --- | --- |
| In-order | 1 3 6 8 10 14 (sorted) |
| Pre-order | 8 3 1 6 10 14 |
| Post-order | 1 6 3 14 10 8 |

### Follow-ups
- *Worst case?* Inserting sorted keys makes a linked list – O(n); use AVL/red-black trees.

**Key idea:** Use `rand()` from `<stdlib.h>`, seeded with `srand()`. `rand()` returns a pseudo-random int in [0, RAND_MAX]; scale it into the range you need. Random inputs are useful to test code over a wide input space.

### Code
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    unsigned seed = (argc > 1) ? (unsigned)atoi(argv[1]) : (unsigned)time(NULL);
    srand(seed);                        /* print the seed so failures can be reproduced */
    printf("seed=%u\n", seed);
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        int r = rand() % 100;           /* 0..99 (slight modulo bias) */
        printf("%d ", r);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

| Function | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `srand(seed)` | set seed (same seed → same sequence) |
| `rand()` | next number in [0, RAND_MAX] |
| `lo + rand() % (hi-lo+1)` | range [lo, hi] |

### Follow-ups
- *SV equivalent?* `$urandom`, `$urandom_range(hi, lo)`, `randomize()` with constraints; seed via simulator `+ntb_random_seed`/`-sv_seed` – always log it for reproducibility.

**Key idea:** **Overloading**: same method name, **different parameter lists**, resolved at compile time (C++, Java – **not supported in SystemVerilog**). **Overriding**: a derived class **redefines a virtual method** of its base with the same signature, resolved at run time (supported in SV).

### Code (C++ overloading)
```cpp
int    add(int a, int b)         { return a + b; }
int    add(int a, int b, int c)  { return a + b + c; }
double add(double a, double b)   { return a + b; }
```

### Code (SystemVerilog overriding)
```systemverilog
class base_pkt;
  bit [7:0] addr;
  virtual function bit compare(base_pkt rhs);
    return (addr == rhs.addr);
  endfunction
endclass

class ext_pkt extends base_pkt;
  bit [31:0] data;
  virtual function bit compare(base_pkt rhs);
    ext_pkt r;
    if (!$cast(r, rhs)) return 0;
    return super.compare(rhs) && (data == r.data);   // reuse + extend
  endfunction
endclass
```

| Aspect | Overloading | Overriding |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Where | same scope/class | base vs derived class |
| Signature | different | same |
| Binding | compile time | run time (virtual) |
| SystemVerilog | no | yes |

### Follow-ups
- *How do you emulate overloading in SV?* Default arguments, or different method names.

**Key idea:** Operator overloading redefines built-in operators (`+`, `==`, `<<`, …) for user-defined types – a form of compile-time polymorphism. Supported in **C++**, not in SystemVerilog (SV uses methods like `compare()`, `copy()`).

### Code (C++)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
class Testclass {
public:
    int a, b;
    Testclass(int a = 0, int b = 0) : a(a), b(b) {}
    Testclass operator+(const Testclass &rhs) const {   // obj1 + obj2
        return Testclass(a + rhs.a, b + rhs.b);
    }
    bool operator==(const Testclass &rhs) const { return a == rhs.a && b == rhs.b; }
};

int main() {
    Testclass x(1, 2), y(3, 4);
    Testclass z = x + y;                 // calls x.operator+(y) → (4, 6)
    std::cout << z.a << "," << z.b << "\n";
}
```

| Expression | Calls |
| --- | --- |
| `x + y` | `x.operator+(y)` |
| `x == y` | `x.operator==(y)` |
| `std::cout << x` | `operator<<(std::cout, x)` (free function) |

### Follow-ups
- *Which operators can't be overloaded in C++?* `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:`, `sizeof`.

**Key idea:** A constructor is a special member function called **automatically when an object is created** to initialize it. In C++ it has the class name; in SystemVerilog it is `function new()`.

### Code
```systemverilog
class packet;
  bit [7:0] addr;
  int id;
  static int count;
  function new(bit [7:0] addr = 0);   // SV constructor (no return type)
    this.addr = addr;
    id = count++;
  endfunction
endclass

class eth_packet extends packet;
  function new(bit [7:0] addr = 8'hFF);
    super.new(addr);                    // must be first statement
  endfunction
endclass
```
```cpp
class Packet { public: int addr; Packet(int a = 0) : addr(a) {} };   // C++ constructor
```

| Aspect | C++ | SystemVerilog |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Name | same as class | `new` |
| Overloads | multiple allowed | one per class (use default args) |
| Base constructor | initializer list | `super.new()` |

### Follow-ups
- *UVM components?* `function new(string name, uvm_component parent)` – build children in `build_phase`, not the constructor.

**Key idea:** A destructor is a special member function invoked automatically when an object is destroyed/goes out of scope, to release resources. C++: `~ClassName()`. SystemVerilog has **no destructor** – memory is garbage collected when no handle references the object.

### Code (C++)
```cpp
#include <cstdio>
class LogFile {
    FILE *fp;
public:
    LogFile(const char *n) { fp = std::fopen(n, "w"); }
    ~LogFile() { if (fp) std::fclose(fp); }    // runs automatically
};
void f() {
    LogFile l("sim.log");
}   // ~LogFile() called here (RAII)
```

| Aspect | C++ | SystemVerilog |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Syntax | `~Class()` | none |
| When | scope exit / `delete` | n/a – GC when last handle is gone |
| Virtual? | make it virtual in base classes | – |

### Follow-ups
- *Release an SV object?* Set all handles to `null`; the simulator reclaims it.

**Key idea:** A virtual function is declared in a base class with `virtual` and can be **overridden** in derived classes; a call through a base pointer/handle dispatches to the **actual object's** version at run time (via a vtable).

### Code (C++)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
struct Base {
    virtual void show() { std::cout << "Base\n"; }
    void plain()        { std::cout << "Base plain\n"; }
    virtual ~Base() = default;
};
struct Derived : Base {
    void show() override { std::cout << "Derived\n"; }
    void plain()         { std::cout << "Derived plain\n"; }
};
int main() {
    Base *p = new Derived;
    p->show();   // Derived   (virtual → dynamic dispatch)
    p->plain();  // Base plain (non-virtual → static binding)
    delete p;
}
```

| Call via base handle | virtual | non-virtual |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Method chosen by | object type (run time) | handle type (compile time) |

### Follow-ups
- *Why a virtual destructor?* So `delete basePtr` runs the derived destructor.
- *SV:* same idea – `virtual function`; once virtual, it stays virtual in derived classes.

**Key idea:** An abstract class contains one or more methods without implementation and **cannot be instantiated**; derived classes must implement them. In SystemVerilog, declare it `virtual class` and use `pure virtual` methods.

### Code (SystemVerilog)
```systemverilog
virtual class BaseShape;
  pure virtual function int get_num_edges();   // no body: must be implemented
endclass

class Rectangle extends BaseShape;
  virtual function int get_num_edges(); return 4; endfunction
endclass

module tb;
  initial begin
    BaseShape s;
    Rectangle r = new();
    // s = new();          // compile error: cannot instantiate an abstract class
    s = r;
    $display("%0d", s.get_num_edges());   // 4
  end
endmodule
```

```mermaid
classDiagram
  BaseShape <|-- Rectangle
  class BaseShape { <<abstract>> +get_num_edges()* }
  class Rectangle { +get_num_edges() }
```

### Follow-ups
- *UVM examples?* `uvm_object`, `uvm_component`, `uvm_sequence_item` are virtual (abstract) base classes.
- *C++?* Any class with a pure virtual function `virtual int f() = 0;` is abstract.
